<h2>Documenting a Water Damage Claim</h2>
<p>Water claims move quickly, and evidence disappears as soon as drying begins. Try to capture:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The source</strong> &ndash; photos of the failed pipe, fitting, hose, or appliance. Keep the failed part if you can.</li>
<li><strong>The spread</strong> &ndash; photos and video of every affected room, including ceilings, walls, baseboards, cabinets, and flooring.</li>
<li><strong>Moisture readings</strong> &ndash; mitigation companies usually record moisture maps and daily readings. Ask for copies.</li>
<li><strong>A plumber's report</strong> &ndash; stating what failed and, if possible, how.</li>
<li><strong>Mitigation invoices</strong> &ndash; extraction, drying equipment, and demolition of wet materials.</li>
<li><strong>A contents list</strong> &ndash; damaged furniture, rugs, electronics, and stored items with ages and replacement prices.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> has more detail.</p>

<h2>Where Water Claims Get Disputed</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>"Long-term leak."</strong> The insurer says water leaked for weeks or months, which many policies exclude.</li>
<li><strong>"Wear and tear."</strong> The failure is blamed on age, corrosion, or lack of maintenance.</li>
<li><strong>Failure to mitigate.</strong> The insurer argues that some damage happened because drying started too late.</li>
<li><strong>Scope.</strong> The estimate dries and patches rather than replacing cabinets or flooring that cannot be dried or matched.</li>
<li><strong>Matching.</strong> Continuous flooring through several rooms may not be matched if only part is replaced.</li>
<li><strong>Mold limits.</strong> Mold remediation may be excluded or capped.</li>
</ul>
<p>If a water claim has been paid low, our <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a> page explains common next steps.</p>

<h2>Local Factors That Shape Water Claims</h2>
<p>Several things about Coastal Bend homes come up again and again on water claims. Many mainland houses are slab-on-grade, so a leak inside a wall or under the slab can spread across flooring in several rooms before anyone sees it. Air conditioning runs most of the year, and attic air handlers with clogged condensate lines are a frequent source of ceiling damage. Elevated homes on Padre Island, Mustang Island, and in Port Aransas often have plumbing running through exposed or lightly insulated spaces, which matters during the rare hard freeze. Older homes in established neighborhoods may have aging supply lines and fittings, which can lead insurers to argue wear and tear. Knowing these patterns helps focus the inspection on the right places and the documentation on the right questions. Our <a href="/services/residential-property-claims/">residential property claims</a> page covers other homeowner claim issues.</p>

<h2>Texas Claim Timelines</h2>
<p>For many insurers, Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542 sets deadlines once a claim is reported: acknowledge the claim and begin the investigation within 15 days, accept or reject within 15 business days after receiving the information requested, and pay within 5 business days after accepting (<a href="https://tcss.legis.texas.gov/resources/IN/htm/IN.542.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tex. Ins. Code Ch. 542</a>). The insurer can extend the decision deadline if it explains why, and deadlines are extended after declared weather catastrophes. Your own policy will also have duties for you, such as prompt notice and, if requested, a proof of loss.</p>

<h2>How a Corpus Christi Public Adjuster May Help</h2>
<p>A Corpus Christi Public Adjuster represents the policyholder only. On a water loss, that may include reviewing the policy for water, mold, and backup provisions; inspecting the property; reviewing mitigation records; preparing a line-item estimate for tear-out, drying, and rebuild; building a contents inventory; and communicating with the insurer's adjuster. We serve Corpus Christi, Nueces County, and surrounding Coastal Bend communities.</p>
<p>We cannot promise any outcome; coverage depends on your policy and the facts. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>Steps to Take Right Away</h2>
<ol>
<li>Shut off the water at the main valve or the fixture.</li>
<li>Turn off electricity to wet areas if it is safe to do so.</li>
<li>Photograph the source and all damage before cleanup.</li>
<li>Report the claim to your insurer promptly.</li>
<li>Call a mitigation company to extract water and start drying; keep the invoices and moisture logs.</li>
<li>Keep failed parts and samples of removed materials when practical.</li>
</ol>
<p>For questions about a lawsuit, bad faith, or deadlines to sue, consult a Texas attorney.</p>
